  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remnants of Isaias bring rain, breezy conditions, \nand an isolated brief tornado or two.\n\nA modified tropical air mass remains entrenched over the area\nwith widespread precipitatable water values ranging from 1.50 to\n2.00 inches. The associated warm conveyor belt has brought\nhighly efficient rainfall to the region with a widespread 1.50\nto 2.50 inches of rain observed during the past 18 hours. This\nincludes some pockets of 3 inch plus amounts where topography\nhas locally augmented the activity. As of 19Z/3 PM, a bulk of\nthese moderate intensity rains have moved north of I-66 and\ncontinue edging toward the Mason-Dixon Line. Off the south, the\nradar presentation suggests a mixture of scattered light showers\nor drizzle. However, this lull is expected to be somewhat brief\nin nature ahead of the main surface circulation which arrives an\nhour or two before dusk.\n\nLooking through the latest 12Z high-resolution models, there\ncontinues to be a signal of heavier rainfall accompanying the\nremnant circulation of Isaias. There appears to be some sort of\ncomma-head feature that contains enhanced lift and moisture\nconvergence. Unlike earlier rainfall elements, this could be a\nbit slower moving and more focused. 3-hour totals could push\ninto the 1 to 2 inch range where this deformation-like band of\nrainfall forms. The focus seems to be somewhere across the\ncentral Virginia Blue Ridge into the adjacent Piedmont region.\nAs such, have opted to maintain the Flood Watch despite the\nmulti-hour lull in between the two rounds.\n\nWhile a non-zero threat for low-topped tornadoes exists, the\noverall probabilities have decreased given the stable air mass\nin place. The Storm Prediction Center shifted the Slight Risk to\nnear I-64 and points southward, with a Marginal Risk extending\nup to the Mason-Dixon Line. Although low-level shear is nearly\noff the charts, the general lack of instability will make for a\ntougher thermodynamic environment to work with. If any\ninstability did materialize, it certainly would not take much\nfor a quick spin up to occur late afternoon into the evening.\n\nThe entire complex of showers will continue to march eastward\ninto the overnight hours. This is largely tied to the upper low\ncurrently swirling back over eastern Kentucky. Until this fully\nclears the region, expect continued low stratus and shower\nchances, along with breezy east/northeasterly winds and\ntemperatures in the 60s. Any chance for sunshine largely will\nwait until midday Monday/Columbus Day.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above normal temperatures through mid week, \nthen a late-week cold front bring a drop in temperatures along \nwith additional precipitation chances.\n\nAfter an active finish to the weekend, subsequent days will\nlikely provide a warming trend along with more sunshine. In\nparticular, Tuesday and Wednesday carry the potential for a\nreturn to the low 80s. There are even hints that some mid 80s\nare possible which seems reasonable as 850-mb temperatures may\nrise to around 16 to 18C. This is all in response to the\nbuilding of heights in the wake of the offshore passage of\nIsaias.\n\nUpper-level ridging will start to transition into zonal flow \nWednesday ahead of an incoming dry cold front, which will cause \ntemperatures to drop on Thursday. However, conditions still remain \ngenerally mild during this time with highs in the low-to-mid 70s \nthrough Friday. While a few rain showers are possible Thursday and \ninto Friday, dry air ahead of and behind the front likely limit \noverall spread & duration. \n\nLooking towards the end of the week, a deep upper-level Canadian \ntrough will traverse the Great Lakes before moving through the East \nCoast. Model guidance varies significantly on timing and impacts of \nthis system. The ECMWF & related ensembles currently has the low \npassing just north of the Mid-Atlantic late Friday and into \nSaturday, whereas the GFS/GEFS shows a much deeper digging trough \nmoving through the Mid-Atlantic throughout the weekend, with a sharp \ndrop in temperatures following it. Given this uncertainty, the \noutlook for the upcoming weekend remains largely uncertain regarding \nany precipitation, although a general cooling trend can likely be \nexpected as the low passes through.",
